## Code Generation Preferences
- TypeScript:
  - Assume **strict** TypeScript; avoid `any` (use `unknown` + narrowing when needed).
  - Prefer explicit return types on exported functions and public helpers when non-trivial.
- Error handling:
  - Use `try/catch/finally` for async flows with `loading` + `error` state.
  - Surface user-facing errors in UI; log developer diagnostics sparingly.
  - Don’t swallow errors; include context (operation + identifiers).
- Comments/docs:
  - Prefer self-documenting code; add comments only for non-obvious logic, edge cases, or security decisions.
  - Keep documentation in `docs/*` for cross-cutting behavior; don’t duplicate large docs in code.
- Components:
  - Always include Tailwind classes (following the ordering convention).
  - Prefer accessible markup (labels, buttons, roles) and include hover/focus states for interactive elements.
- Styling utilities:
  - Use `cn()` for conditional classes.
  - Use **CVA** for component variants when a component has multiple style variants (e.g., `variant`, `size`, `intent`).
  - Use **tailwind-merge** to resolve class conflicts (especially with variants and conditionals).

## Forbidden Patterns
- No new component-level CSS files.
- No inline `style={{}}` props (except last resort; document why).
- No CSS-in-JS (`styled-components`, `@emotion/*`, etc.).
- No `@apply` except inside the shared base/global styles (`packages/ui-theme/styles.css`).
- No `!important` in Tailwind classes.
- No custom CSS classes when a Tailwind utility exists.
- No hardcoded color/spacing values when theme tokens exist (use configured theme colors/spacing).
- No external state libraries (Redux, Zustand, MobX, Recoil) unless explicitly approved (project uses React built-ins).
- No server-state libraries (React Query/SWR/Apollo) unless explicitly approved (project uses manual fetching via `services/*`).
- Don’t introduce heavy utility libraries (e.g. lodash) without strong justification.

## Component Generation Template
When creating a new component:
- Import `cn` if you need conditional classes.
- Define a `Props` interface in TypeScript.
- Use a function component (`export function ComponentName(props: Props) { ... }`).
- Apply Tailwind classes in the required class-ordering convention.
- Add responsive classes (mobile-first) where appropriate.
- Add hover/focus/active/disabled states for interactive elements.
